(* =============================================================================================
   §H Holes

   None. No byte, value or code in this file waits on an M0 measurement. The file freezes with
   the query surface after WP-72; a remedy of WP-73 that changes a spelling, a refusal or a
   production's content is a specification edit before the tag, not a hole fill (§O O-11).
   ============================================================================================= *)


(* =============================================================================================
   §O Open points for the review

   O-1  Undefined names in [50 §2.3]. label, type_name, letter, digit, lower, hex, hex32,
        exponent, char, escape, any, yyyy, mm, dd, hh and ss are used but not defined. Annex A
        defines them as character classes and aliases, outside the 82 counted productions.
   O-2  REF@{n}. [50 §2.4] accepts git's REF@{n} "in files", but [50 §2.3]'s rev_suffix does not
        derive it. Resolution: a fourth alternative '@' '{' int '}' with the meaning of '@' int;
        the production count is unchanged. Braces are only safe in files, heredocs and MCP
        strings ([80 §4] T3), which is where LQ text travels.
   O-3  "HEAD" is double-quoted in [50]'s rev_base, which the notation makes case-insensitive,
        but head is a valid ref_word. Resolution: HEAD is exact upper case in revision mode; any
        other upper-case letter in a revision is E003 ([LQ/lexical §7.2]).
   O-4  violations. [50 §2.2] rule 6 lists violations among the relations with revision-typed
        arguments, while [50 §2.6] shows violations() with none. Resolution: the revision
        position is positional argument 0 or the named argument ref (as the design's probe
        parser lqcheck2.py has it). [LQ/std §2.8] item 1 and §2.9 adopt it: violations takes the
        optional argument ref: rev, the staging ref to read, equal to a USE of that ref.
   O-5  changes(ref:). [50 §2.6] gives changes(since: rev, ref: ...). Resolution: ref is a
        revision position too ([LQ/lexical §4.2]), so ref: lane/x needs no quotes; the probe
        parser had since only. [LQ/std §2.8] item 1 adopts ref: rev.
   O-6  Refused spellings added beyond [50 §2.8]'s table, each with the code the design's probe
        parser used or the closest code of [50 §5.2]: nodes( and relationships( (E004, [50
        §2.3.1] lists nodes()/edges() as not in v1); <-[...]-> (E004); CREATE/DROP INDEX and
        CONSTRAINT (E004 rather than E006); a CALL of a non-tx name inside TX (E109, as the probe
        parser did); Cypher's WITH order (E001 with rewrite); the empty subquery (E001); TX { }
        (E009 rather than E001). [LQ/errors] carries the texts.
   O-7  E118 scope. The probe parser raised E118 only for =, <> and != against NULL; P18 keeps
        that and leaves ordered comparisons with NULL to the binder (a type error there).
   O-8  Edge variables on a quantified edge (-[e:T*1..3]->, -[e:T]->+) parse; LQ has no list or
        path values, so the binder refuses them (E113, hint: a quantified group
        ((a)-[e:T]->(b) WHERE ...){1,3}). The rule belongs to the binder's error table
        ([LQ/errors]); recorded here because the grammar admits the form.
   O-9  Strict-GQL mode. [50] names the alternative surface "LQ with strict GQL spellings" but
        does not define it. Annex G defines it as a parser flag over this grammar. Open for
        WP-70/WP-72: whether the "Cypher-spelling tolerance off" ablation uses the same flag
        (this file assumes it does) and which card each arm gets.
   O-10 Named-argument names are case-sensitive (P10), as parameter names are ([50 §2.2] rule 4);
        the probe parser lower-cased them.
   O-11 Freeze with remedies. [50 §7.4] item 6 lets a failed GT13 gate change "the compatibility
        table, or the semantics" before the freeze, so Annex R, Annex G and the content of a
        production may still change through WP-73; the production names and the count of
        Annex C stay. That is a freeze condition, not a measured value, so it is not a named
        hole ([F01 §2.5]). After the tag, a change is a new grammar version, and definitions
        stored under version 1 keep version-1 syntax and semantics ([50 §7.4] item 6).
   O-12 PLAN gap "Canonical-AST encoding; JSON IR schema" ([PLAN §3.3], WP-19) is resolved in
        [LQ/canonical-ast] and [LQ/json-ir]; this file supplies the productions they cite.
   O-13 Two GQL spellings outside v1 are refused with their LQ form rather than a generic E001:
        IS LABELED (write x:a) and CAST(x AS T) (write toInteger, toFloat or toString), as
        [LQ/errors] open point 12 asked. LABELED and CAST are refused words
        ([LQ/lexical §6.4]); elsewhere they are names.
   O-14 The design's probe parser (lqcheck2.py and its 47 fixtures, [50 §11]; unpublished probe
        scripts, not in the repository) was read as evidence of [50]'s intent. This file follows
        it (the '<-' rule, Cypher quantifier bounds, E109 for a non-tx CALL inside TX, E113 for a
        path variable) except where listed: E004 for nodes( and relationships(, which the probe
        left to the binder (O-6); case-sensitive named arguments (O-10); changes(ref:) as a
        revision position (O-5).
   ============================================================================================= *)
